<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Miyoshi Nagayoshi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1522">1522</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Miyoshi Chōkei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Miyoshi Nagayoshi was a Japanese warlord of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku period</a> who dominated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> and the home provinces in the 1550s. A retainer of the Hosokawa deputy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shoguns</a>, he turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-harumoto">Hosokawa Harumoto</a> in 1549, defeated him at Eguchi and drove the shogun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiteru">Ashikaga Yoshiteru</a> from the capital. His regime is sometimes seen as the first unifying power in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/miyoshi-nagayoshi.jpg" alt="Miyoshi Nagayoshi" width="320" height="698" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 投稿者がファイル作成 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E4%B8%89%E5%A5%BD%E9%95%B7%E6%85%B6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
